out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{70}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{70})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "At $t=0,$ the supplied model gives $Q(0)=\\alpha(1-0/70)+\\beta(1-0/70)^{3}=\\alpha+\\beta.$ The initial amount is $33.6\\,\\text{kg},$ so $\\alpha+\\beta=33.6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04084bfe-728e-56a7-9b51-18222c30b437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672768} {:answer/id #uuid "3dae683b-2e03-5c8e-a158-f7f7c7fda8c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672775} {:answer/id #uuid "9f109f9c-f530-5225-bb20-d5d4b5ed6492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672787} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1b1fc9-615a-5a34-838a-56c9ba93c0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672788} {:answer/id #uuid "f60d354d-5417-538b-ba01-242c60b7ea1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60d354d-5417-538b-ba01-242c60b7ea1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672802} :answer-field/id #uuid "c730bc48-ce6a-592f-8bf9-706c89365176" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4cc01cd-a72e-561d-b122-3c7198986a9c" :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $500L$ of water containing $34kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.02kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $20L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{50}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{50})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "At $t=0,$ the supplied model gives $Q(0)=\\alpha(1-0/50)+\\beta(1-0/50)^{3}=\\alpha+\\beta.$ The initial amount is $34\\,\\text{kg},$ so $\\alpha+\\beta=34.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e1e846-9e05-501b-974e-8642dacc9f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "228.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672808} {:answer/id #uuid "96899a85-e81a-5fa2-9faf-596f72428877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "224.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672824} {:answer/id #uuid "9c03c3f7-70d4-5faa-9e3b-ecaa003c3baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "226.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672826} {:answer/id #uuid "ca007285-9488-5d69-931c-3c1cdba5a712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "223.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672833} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0dd9a1-8939-534b-8fe1-61130a258c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff0dd9a1-8939-534b-8fe1-61130a258c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672837} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8238105-55b4-50ef-b094-4a132b322bef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4d24b72e-d25f-59d2-bb3e-c6bd67f97b9c" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $190\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =18\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=50+15t-10t=5(10+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q=18.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(10+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|10+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(10+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q) & =(10+t)^{2}\\cdot 18 \\\\ (10+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(10+t)Q & =18(10+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2}) & =18(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 18(10+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(10+t)^{2} & =18\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(10+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =6(10+t)+\\frac{C}{(10+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ 6(10+0)+\\frac{C}{(10+0)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ 60+\\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =-40 \\\\ C & =-40(10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =6(10+t)-\\frac{40(10)^{2}}{(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =6(10+t)-40{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $190\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=190$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =190 \\\\ 50+15{t}_{o}-10{t}_{o} & =190 \\\\ 50+5{t}_{o} & =190 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =140 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =28\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(28) & =6(10+28)-40{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+28}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =228-40{\\left(\\frac{10}{38}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈225.2\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d3dbfe-81e6-5c4b-bc37-f60fb4beb15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "238.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672810} {:answer/id #uuid "3845663d-6c8b-564f-991e-5525391b388d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "241.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672811} {:answer/id #uuid "9505e145-3829-51e6-af55-4ca05fb593e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "242.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672823} {:answer/id #uuid "983c0311-04b0-5af4-b7ae-1d85c98e4775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672825} {:answer/id #uuid "9c112657-3a58-5227-8060-681920d3d89d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "239.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c112657-3a58-5227-8060-681920d3d89d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "239.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672827} :answer-field/id #uuid "53445c49-a61b-5717-8a8f-2ba5cfb737e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "566ddc14-35df-5f2a-8371-ba5cac1be4c4" :question/problem "An ink reservoir for an industrial printer can hold up to $249\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of ink solution with $80\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved ink dye. A more concentrated ink solution with concentration $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir minus the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of ink dye enters the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *ink dye* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+9t-6t=3(40+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(40+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *ink dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(40+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(40+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(40+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{40+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{40+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{40+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(40+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|40+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(40+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(40+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(40+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(40+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{40+t}\\,Q) & =(40+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (40+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(40+t)Q & =9(40+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40+t)^{2}) & =9(40+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(40+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(40+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(40+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(40+t)+\\frac{C}{(40+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $80\\,\\text{lb}$ of ink dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=80,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =80 \\\\ 3(40+0)+\\frac{C}{(40+0)^{2}} & =80 \\\\ 120+\\frac{C}{(40)^{2}} & =80 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{40}^{2}} & =-40 \\\\ C & =-40(40)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(40+t)-\\frac{40(40)^{2}}{(40+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(40+t)-40{\\left(\\frac{40}{40+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $249\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=249$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =249 \\\\ 120+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =249 \\\\ 120+3{t}_{o} & =249 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =129 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =43\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(43) & =3(40+43)-40{\\left(\\frac{40}{40+43}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =249-40{\\left(\\frac{40}{83}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈239.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4a3eff-430a-5254-b33c-e4b7a1ce9007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "383.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672806} {:answer/id #uuid "25f1ca40-bbb2-585e-b802-e7abc4b0609a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "381.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672809} {:answer/id #uuid "3f66ff27-185c-5ed0-968e-616134064bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "379.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672812} {:answer/id #uuid "700b78b2-e9d9-511c-b04d-6cf0070d3cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "378.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672819} {:answer/id #uuid "dafbce03-3f81-585d-94d3-c444a4c0b2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "382.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25f1ca40-bbb2-585e-b802-e7abc4b0609a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "381.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672809} :answer-field/id #uuid "0939b42c-050b-5b2d-a3aa-812a8cbc4426" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b9bb014c-8036-50d5-98f7-cb585fa07997" :question/problem "A dye mixing tank at a textile factory can hold up to $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution with $120\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved dye. A more concentrated dye solution with concentration $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =36\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $30\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=300+30t-20t=10(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{10(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{10(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{10(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{10(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =36-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =36(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =36(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 36(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =36\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $120\\,\\text{lb}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=120,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =120 \\\\ 12(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =120 \\\\ 360+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =120 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =-240 \\\\ C & =-240(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(30+t)-\\frac{240(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =12(30+t)-240{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $420\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=420$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =420 \\\\ 300+30{t}_{o}-20{t}_{o} & =420 \\\\ 300+10{t}_{o} & =420 \\\\ 10{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =12\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(12) & =12(30+12)-240{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+12}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =504-240{\\left(\\frac{30}{42}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈381.6\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50c05219-e146-5c0b-9f47-8478e9cc17eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "73.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672815} {:answer/id #uuid "58e5327d-e065-5983-b7c0-80260b2d7837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "77.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672818} {:answer/id #uuid "79055611-d13a-5b4e-b215-47c495ddcd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672820} {:answer/id #uuid "815ad045-92f6-5634-9a68-0ba13cc2bea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672822} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cc719f-91cb-5ba1-9d7a-0694870d0ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "815ad045-92f6-5634-9a68-0ba13cc2bea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672822} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f0a520-4075-560e-ac9e-5f9c2b76fd9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c4c7ec47-5290-53ac-8dc7-8af5327e3c22" :question/problem "A coolant reservoir in a manufacturing plant can hold up to $184\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of coolant solution with $15\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved corrosion inhibitor. A more concentrated coolant solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir minus the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of inhibitor enters the reservoir at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *inhibitor* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+12t-8t=4(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *inhibitor* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =6(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =6(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 6(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $15\\,\\text{lb}$ of inhibitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ 2(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =15 \\\\ 60+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =15 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{30}^{2}} & =-45 \\\\ C & =-45(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2(30+t)-\\frac{45(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2(30+t)-45{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $184\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=184$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =184 \\\\ 120+12{t}_{o}-8{t}_{o} & =184 \\\\ 120+4{t}_{o} & =184 \\\\ 4{t}_{o} & =64 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =16\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(16) & =2(30+16)-45{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+16}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =92-45{\\left(\\frac{30}{46}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈72.9\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144981e1-0af5-524f-b755-723ec92ceafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "497.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672805} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9ed5b4-4935-583e-a40d-801dceb367ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "493.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672807} {:answer/id #uuid "80951659-772c-51af-88a0-1b9a88f45896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "495.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672821} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f73285-229e-5601-bd7e-d317529e0561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "495.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672829} {:answer/id #uuid "ed696f17-3d6d-50f3-a6d4-1a3ad80e8f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "496.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80951659-772c-51af-88a0-1b9a88f45896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "495.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672821} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6e5f789-32c8-5623-8da5-2970434753ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ff58189c-6e25-5dd9-8cd1-371a00326207" :question/problem "A fertilizer tank for an irrigation system can hold up to $352\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $176\\,\\text{gal}$ of fertilizer solution with $132\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved fertilizer. A more concentrated solution with concentration $1.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $16\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of fertilizer enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *fertilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =36\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $176\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $16\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=176+24t-16t=8(22+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{8(22+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *fertilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{8(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{22+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =36-\\frac{2Q}{22+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{22+t}\\,Q=36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(22+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|22+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(22+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(22+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(22+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(22+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{22+t}\\,Q) & =(22+t)^{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\ (22+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(22+t)Q & =36(22+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(22+t)^{2}) & =36(22+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(22+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 36(22+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(22+t)^{2} & =36\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(22+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(22+t)+\\frac{C}{(22+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $132\\,\\text{lb}$ of fertilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=132,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =132 \\\\ 12(22+0)+\\frac{C}{(22+0)^{2}} & =132 \\\\ 264+\\frac{C}{(22)^{2}} & =132 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{22}^{2}} & =-132 \\\\ C & =-132(22)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(22+t)-\\frac{132(22)^{2}}{(22+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =12(22+t)-132{\\left(\\frac{22}{22+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $352\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=352$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =352 \\\\ 176+24{t}_{o}-16{t}_{o} & =352 \\\\ 176+8{t}_{o} & =352 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =176 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =22\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(22) & =12(22+22)-132{\\left(\\frac{22}{22+22}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =528-132{\\left(\\frac{22}{44}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈495.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247aea16-5a0e-53c2-9eab-769593e0321b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10.8-4.8e^{-t/30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672842} {:answer/id #uuid "4a75fa10-6da7-5407-9c8d-f142fc65c9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=18-8e^{-t/24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672848} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf6c63e-69eb-5222-be8b-10c3f1be8e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=18-8e^{-t/30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672850} {:answer/id #uuid "9294db4d-c049-5c7e-9775-3e4ab32bdfb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10.8-0.8e^{-t/24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672860} {:answer/id #uuid "d583b770-9481-5579-8d91-684112cd4467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=18-12e^{-t/24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "247aea16-5a0e-53c2-9eab-769593e0321b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10.8-4.8e^{-t/30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672842} :answer-field/id #uuid "f72c24e8-2705-5501-b569-59c87bcb3ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1b80a0f4-7801-535e-bc20-eae6ddee5773" :question/problem "A brewing tank initially holds $360\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of sugar $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.36\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $360\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{360}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{360}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{30}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.36-\\frac{Q}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{30}Q=0.36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{30}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/30},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{30}\\alpha =0.36\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =10.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=10.8.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/30}+10.8.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/30}+10.8 & =6 \\\\ A & =-4.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=10.8-4.8{e}^{-t/30}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2672e6b2-eafa-5b83-9c60-d31c4e3baaed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=14-2e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672843} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6606fe-295f-54d9-a96b-ece95eaa67cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=21-3e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672852} {:answer/id #uuid "a57aeafe-7041-5c00-8fd6-c39c93acb22f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=14+4e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672863} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e7feec-a4a9-5925-8994-9b16f6261677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=21-3e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672864} {:answer/id #uuid "f524c191-98bd-54bf-8c61-18cfacadfeed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=21-9e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7e7feec-a4a9-5925-8994-9b16f6261677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=21-3e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672864} :answer-field/id #uuid "669474bd-f365-5bcf-8161-9f793e84c972" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "387eb92e-520a-5684-94a0-8f6b898f8d01" :question/problem "A storage tank initially holds $350\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant.\nA feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the disinfectant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of disinfectant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of disinfectant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the tank minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the disinfectant enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $350\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=1.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =1.5\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =21.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=21.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+21.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+21 & =18 \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=21-3{e}^{-t/14}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a120c58-a242-55df-ae6b-10b365f315f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=16-e^{-t/80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672847} {:answer/id #uuid "63be0667-c58b-5165-b1b6-6499626e2f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=16+9e^{-t/16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672853} {:answer/id #uuid "74120287-fbd3-5aed-838e-a58eb85a7813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=24+e^{-t/80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672856} {:answer/id #uuid "9d11c0ba-204a-532b-a292-0fbe25eff11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=24-9e^{-t/16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672862} {:answer/id #uuid "e0be752c-5cae-56aa-a99b-5e2e3da84b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=24-9e^{-t/80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0be752c-5cae-56aa-a99b-5e2e3da84b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=24-9e^{-t/80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672871} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cab0c71-5a81-5ddc-9dfe-02c242f74b7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "79b642d6-727a-5f0d-9531-4fcd626dce40" :question/problem "A coolant tank initially holds $800\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive.\nA feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive is pumped into the tank at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of additive $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of additive in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additive enters the tank minus the rate at which additive leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additive enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive enters the tank at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *additive* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additive exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $800\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{80}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.3-\\frac{Q}{80}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80}Q=0.3.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/80},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{80}\\alpha =0.3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=24.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/80}+24.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/80}+24 & =15 \\\\ A & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=24-9{e}^{-t/80}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f327130-ce5a-5579-9f17-3c81140d18c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=60-46e^{-t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672851} {:answer/id #uuid "9071fcc3-5fc2-5104-a059-5fc677809145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10+4e^{-t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672858} {:answer/id #uuid "91d23fe2-88cf-561c-a0e4-1741996358a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10+5e^{-t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672859} {:answer/id #uuid "ae63b870-5af2-55d8-954f-51150459ff29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10+5e^{-t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672866} {:answer/id #uuid "f23c15db-b163-510c-9170-9872773ac7af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=60-45e^{-t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae63b870-5af2-55d8-954f-51150459ff29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10+5e^{-t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672866} :answer-field/id #uuid "31456c23-1619-532c-936a-7b4aab6fde98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bd866fe0-d825-5d93-8387-02823cb34e5f" :question/problem "A brewing tank initially holds $1000\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA feed solution containing $0.01\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of sugar $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.01\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.25\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $1000\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{1000}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{1000}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{1000}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{1000}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{40}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.25-\\frac{Q}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}Q=0.25.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{40}\\alpha =0.25\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=10.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40}+10.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/40}+10 & =15 \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=10+5{e}^{-t/40}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44b353c1-c2ee-54c3-b666-ccb5c859c5f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=8.4-4.4e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672846} {:answer/id #uuid "67f70dc6-1daf-5923-90ad-76fd65eed828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=25.2-20.2e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672855} {:answer/id #uuid "996a9731-acc5-5b69-8d22-f2fd2ac623d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=25.2-20.2e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672861} {:answer/id #uuid "ae638f6f-9aab-5257-8412-79161e72772d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=8.4-3.4e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672865} {:answer/id #uuid "d9dc6910-635d-50c4-b176-6fbb2ac614b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=25.2-21.2e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44b353c1-c2ee-54c3-b666-ccb5c859c5f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=8.4-4.4e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672846} :answer-field/id #uuid "23514375-6fae-594a-b5a7-0e55f8d76eee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d3e93811-bde2-50b4-a162-641048dbfc47" :question/problem "A coolant tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive.\nA feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of additive $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of additive in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additive enters the tank minus the rate at which additive leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additive enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *additive* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additive exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $420\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =8.4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=8.4.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+8.4.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=4,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =4 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+8.4 & =4 \\\\ A & =-4.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=8.4-4.4{e}^{-t/14}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c33ed24b-0e50-5e68-b7c9-51636be8a916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{t^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{t^{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186677835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c33ed24b-0e50-5e68-b7c9-51636be8a916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{t^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{t^{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186677835} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6ecff0b-510f-53d9-9ce2-7bf80bd5c585" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186677837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "075fc33b-6832-5788-a640-e15159a0d53b" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int \\sqrt[4]{t}-t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $t>0$." :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\sqrt[4]{t}-t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(t^{\\frac{1}{4}}-t^{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-\\int t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{5}{4}}}{\\frac{5}{4}}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{t^{5}}{5}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{t^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{t^{5}}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9293bf01-5e59-5f05-bbc0-2f956d07ddac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t^{3}}{3}-\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{t^{5}}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186677833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9293bf01-5e59-5f05-bbc0-2f956d07ddac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t^{3}}{3}-\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{t^{5}}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186677833} :answer-field/id #uuid "c96efe24-a3df-5b4b-a44c-9a0fb7a95563" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186677836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b3249088-74ad-54eb-8ed2-53ba020d6811" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int -t^{2}-\\sqrt[4]{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $t>0$." :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -t^{2}-\\sqrt[4]{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-t^{2}-t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -\\int t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-\\int t^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -\\left(\\frac{t^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{5}{4}}}{\\frac{5}{4}}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{t^{3}}{3}-\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{t^{5}}}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067e2dcf-3446-50d6-8bc0-12de978f5c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 5, 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678559} {:answer/id #uuid "5feafdea-a285-50f0-a9c3-2f218ef8fc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 1, 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678568} {:answer/id #uuid "bb30a34d-caf0-524d-890e-8c6c020aabe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 3, 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678579} {:answer/id #uuid "bde7cdb7-3f4f-5dde-8802-5f7900895a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 2, 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678580} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c0d90d-d4b9-5fcb-bdb0-20b731af6227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 2, 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5feafdea-a285-50f0-a9c3-2f218ef8fc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 1, 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678568} :answer-field/id #uuid "5818a3e6-e7e7-5a4c-96cc-3ee21c2148bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "344170ea-fccc-525e-b59d-a216f9f643f0" :question/problem "Solve $-z^{2} - 3z + 15 = -2z^{2} + 4z + 9$." :question/worked-solution "First, bring all terms to the left side and collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-z^{2} - 3z + 15 &= -2z^{2} + 4z + 9 \\\\\n(-z^{2} - 3z + 15) - (-2z^{2} + 4z + 9) &= 0 \\\\\nz^{2} - 7z + 6 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor, find two numbers that multiply to $6$ and add to $-7$. They are $-1$ and $-6$. Split the linear term and factor by grouping.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} - 7z + 6 &= 0 \\\\\nz^{2} + (-1)z + (-6)z + (6) &= 0 \\\\\n(z^{2} + (-1)z) + ((-6)z + (6)) &= 0 \\\\\nz(z - 1) + (-6)(z - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(z - 1)(z - 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - 1 &= 0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad z - 6 = 0 \\\\\nz &= 1 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad z = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z = 1, 6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0328575f-adee-5320-977f-c0c2703166e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = -1, 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678558} {:answer/id #uuid "5f656fd6-1ecc-565f-877f-6927d403065e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = -7, 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678567} {:answer/id #uuid "6d35ac70-c67b-5d57-8fad-67f5cb6ebcbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = -2, 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678570} {:answer/id #uuid "acc7f6df-44c9-54ee-8ce8-88f852d54a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = -1, 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678578} {:answer/id #uuid "dec4f937-d198-5b6e-92a3-e5899cd6e755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = -4, 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acc7f6df-44c9-54ee-8ce8-88f852d54a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = -1, 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678578} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ae41d11-6635-5c83-a339-839fb0878438" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "84afa59e-66b6-528e-890f-a7175f819158" :question/problem "Solve $x^{2} - 2x + 13 = 2x^{2} - 3x + 11$." :question/worked-solution "First, bring all terms to the left side and collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 13 &= 2x^{2} - 3x + 11 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 2x + 13) - (2x^{2} - 3x + 11) &= 0 \\\\\n-x^{2} + x + 2 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply both sides by $-1$ so that the leading coefficient is positive.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-1)(-x^{2} + x + 2) &= (-1)(0) \\\\\nx^{2} - x - 2 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor, find two numbers that multiply to $-2$ and add to $-1$. They are $1$ and $-2$. Split the linear term and factor by grouping.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - x - 2 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + (1)x + (-2)x + (-2) &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + (1)x) + ((-2)x + (-2)) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 1) + (-2)(x + 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)(x - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= 0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x - 2 = 0 \\\\\nx &= -1 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x = -1, 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13529464-c87a-53d3-a76e-6e17cbd3e0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = -4, -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678562} {:answer/id #uuid "959a23c6-08d4-560d-ac76-e0a603e8c887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = -8, -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678576} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c25b13-623f-54e8-958c-82abfb4855c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = -5, -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678583} {:answer/id #uuid "dbea1080-6872-5810-9cb3-cdb88c7cb4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = -9, -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678586} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9e1446-921d-52c3-8084-ff356ac68c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = -5, -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbea1080-6872-5810-9cb3-cdb88c7cb4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = -9, -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678586} :answer-field/id #uuid "73bf5a50-3e9c-5665-a028-99b90413a80f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2b53c65-6f9c-5413-9bf8-1e915488d045" :question/problem "Solve $y^{2} + 15y + 22 = 2y - 14$." :question/worked-solution "First, bring all terms to the left side and collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} + 15y + 22 &= 2y - 14 \\\\\n(y^{2} + 15y + 22) - (2y - 14) &= 0 \\\\\ny^{2} + 13y + 36 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor, find two numbers that multiply to $36$ and add to $13$. They are $9$ and $4$. Split the linear term and factor by grouping.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} + 13y + 36 &= 0 \\\\\ny^{2} + (9)y + (4)y + (36) &= 0 \\\\\n(y^{2} + (9)y) + ((4)y + (36)) &= 0 \\\\\ny(y + 9) + (4)(y + 9) &= 0 \\\\\n(y + 9)(y + 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny + 9 &= 0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad y + 4 = 0 \\\\\ny &= -9 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad y = -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y = -9, -4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e4b952-6a0a-53b9-b243-c1ac1c715a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = -4, -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678561} {:answer/id #uuid "704b4502-6dee-58f5-9e15-48232e9d2147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = -7, -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678571} {:answer/id #uuid "7616fae6-79c8-5263-b44a-3da083de9a6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = -9, -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678573} {:answer/id #uuid "97bf2465-c6b5-5e31-aea9-9ea8c3c117e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = -3, -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678577} {:answer/id #uuid "cecc7878-5894-5a62-b9f5-80117a1d6e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = -5, -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "704b4502-6dee-58f5-9e15-48232e9d2147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = -7, -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678571} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0da7c7-1e23-5928-8e4c-db5ef40ea3f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c5bb5ee3-d1fd-592e-8f74-9a8baa1148a6" :question/problem "Solve $-x^{2} - 6x - 29 = 4x - 8$." :question/worked-solution "First, bring all terms to the left side and collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} - 6x - 29 &= 4x - 8 \\\\\n(-x^{2} - 6x - 29) - (4x - 8) &= 0 \\\\\n-x^{2} - 10x - 21 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply both sides by $-1$ so that the leading coefficient is positive.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-1)(-x^{2} - 10x - 21) &= (-1)(0) \\\\\nx^{2} + 10x + 21 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor, find two numbers that multiply to $21$ and add to $10$. They are $7$ and $3$. Split the linear term and factor by grouping.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 10x + 21 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + (7)x + (3)x + (21) &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + (7)x) + ((3)x + (21)) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 7) + (3)(x + 7) &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 7)(x + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 7 &= 0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x + 3 = 0 \\\\\nx &= -7 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x = -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x = -7, -3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186678587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ce750b-99cd-5cf6-aa2f-8793fd81db8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 4, 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678557} {:answer/id #uuid "0e35e4f4-1636-5c53-9f55-e4e84378f4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 2, 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678560} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe8c47b-d562-5eb5-9bcf-0c01f570d745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 2, 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678564} {:answer/id #uuid "67515400-a941-50a1-9d6e-9acc6f9bb6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 2, 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678569} {:answer/id #uuid "c115850d-5db8-5f81-a0d3-4a0fba283c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 2, 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67515400-a941-50a1-9d6e-9acc6f9bb6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = 2, 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678569} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf1e65f-cbd9-5f7b-b8a7-62ddac995c0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186678588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc28b462-0ffc-56ee-8c95-1e5583d20e5b" :question/problem "Solve $z^{2} - 10z + 2 = -2z - 10$." :question/worked-solution "First, bring all terms to the left side and collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} - 10z + 2 &= -2z - 10 \\\\\n(z^{2} - 10z + 2) - (-2z - 10) &= 0 \\\\\nz^{2} - 8z + 12 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor, find two numbers that multiply to $12$ and add to $-8$. They are $-2$ and $-6$. Split the linear term and factor by grouping.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} - 8z + 12 &= 0 \\\\\nz^{2} + (-2)z + (-6)z + (12) &= 0 \\\\\n(z^{2} + (-2)z) + ((-6)z + (12)) &= 0 \\\\\nz(z - 2) + (-6)(z - 2) &= 0 \\\\\n(z - 2)(z - 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - 2 &= 0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad z - 6 = 0 \\\\\nz &= 2 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad z = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z = 2, 6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c95a787-73e0-53e0-a2bc-fd2f5c520a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{x^{3}}-\\frac{2x^{3}}{3}+4+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680880} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb7024c-0c82-515b-bcd5-ad57b3bbc5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{x^{3}}+\\frac{2x^{3}}{3}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680882} {:answer/id #uuid "56840ca7-616b-5c9c-9749-4b1997c49228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{x^{3}}}{2}-\\frac{2x^{3}}{3}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680884} {:answer/id #uuid "99573cbe-11f8-5be8-b1bb-abfae0590262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{x^{3}}-\\frac{2x^{3}}{3}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680891} {:answer/id #uuid "e3be435d-9efa-52dd-9c61-dbf33fa8d774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{x^{3}}-2x^{3}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99573cbe-11f8-5be8-b1bb-abfae0590262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{x^{3}}-\\frac{2x^{3}}{3}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680891} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b273a10-e35d-5655-bcb3-3800e3205664" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "819846e6-bc40-53d0-9281-b1774d161401" :question/problem "Calculate the indefinite integral for $x>0$.\n\n$$\n\\int (3\\sqrt{x}-2x^{2}+4)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Rewrite the radical as a power: $\\sqrt{x}=x^{1/2}$. Then use the sum, constant factor, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\int (3\\sqrt{x}-2x^{2}+4)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\n&=3\\int x^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-2\\int x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+4\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\n&=3\\left(\\frac{x^{3/2}}{3/2}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)+4x+C \\\\\n&=2x^{3/2}-\\frac{2x^{3}}{3}+4x+C \\\\\n&=2\\sqrt{x^{3}}-\\frac{2x^{3}}{3}+4x+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $3/2$ multiplies by $2/3$. For $x>0$, $x^{3/2}=\\sqrt{x^{3}}$. Differentiating the result gives $3\\sqrt{x}-2x^{2}+4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aa1e6a4-44ca-59c7-a559-74398283496e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{3}-x^{2}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680883} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5cb199-f7ca-5f9d-bd10-2fd49abe6fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}-x^{2}+4+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680885} {:answer/id #uuid "605915e4-484c-5f14-82bb-77ffefa4ac3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}-2x^{2}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680886} {:answer/id #uuid "878e53ed-3c6b-5a25-aca9-6d4fd86a336d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}+x^{2}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680890} {:answer/id #uuid "d7086787-9065-55c4-9027-98eb6867477f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}-x^{2}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7086787-9065-55c4-9027-98eb6867477f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}-x^{2}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680897} :answer-field/id #uuid "376eee18-d597-518c-a31e-4aa80a894e39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b86dd312-0882-5996-8023-e03328823cb1" :question/problem "Calculate the indefinite integral.\n\n$$\n\\int (3x^{2}-2x+4)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the sum and constant factor rules to integrate each term. For each power, add one to the exponent and divide by the new exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\int (3x^{2}-2x+4)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\n&=3\\int x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-2\\int x\\,\\mathrm{d}x+4\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\n&=3\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)+4x+C \\\\\n&=x^{3}-x^{2}+4x+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant term integrates to $4x$, and the arbitrary constants combine into one $C$. Differentiating $x^{3}-x^{2}+4x+C$ gives $3x^{2}-2x+4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb57791-746c-57d4-ae17-9b7ce25d4505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}-x^{3}-\\frac{4}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680879} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2ff63c-3b6f-5e48-92fd-a6bbd4631fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}-3x^{3}-\\frac{4}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680887} {:answer/id #uuid "836450ab-8281-5391-a5a9-5a354f0729c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}-x^{3}+\\frac{4}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680889} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ecfa91-4b36-5a28-ab8c-3858d392c177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{x}}-x^{3}-\\frac{4}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680893} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b970a4-7080-592a-8969-6926f8811729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}-x^{3}-\\frac{4}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0eb57791-746c-57d4-ae17-9b7ce25d4505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}-x^{3}-\\frac{4}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680879} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3c87629-4d42-5af3-9ce2-43baaf7ee2ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fe02c1b7-b222-56b6-a00c-48f7d3abd8ce" :question/problem "Calculate the indefinite integral for $x>0$.\n\n$$\n\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{x^{4}}}-3x^{2}+\\frac{4}{x^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write the reciprocal powers as $2x^{-4/3}$ and $4x^{-2}$. The sum and constant factor rules give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{x^{4}}}-3x^{2}+\\frac{4}{x^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\n&=2\\int x^{-4/3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-3\\int x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+4\\int x^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\n&=2\\left(\\frac{x^{-1/3}}{-1/3}\\right)-3\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)+4\\left(\\frac{x^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C \\\\\n&=-6x^{-1/3}-x^{3}-4x^{-1}+C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}-x^{3}-\\frac{4}{x}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $-4/3+1=-1/3$ and $-2+1=-1$. Dividing by these negative exponents makes both reciprocal contributions negative. As a check, differentiating the result gives $2x^{-4/3}-3x^{2}+4x^{-2}$, the original integrand."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186681140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28dbb091-f1c8-535f-93e4-ff0cd0ad3a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{t^{3}}}{3}-t^{3}-3t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681139} {:answer/id #uuid "d29bc728-ba4b-5544-ba12-93dee556ff33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{t^{3}}}{3}-2t^{3}-3t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681141} {:answer/id #uuid "d42b1edd-ff40-5b87-86b4-e3a728f2757f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{t^{3}}-2t^{3}-3t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681142} {:answer/id #uuid "f6407384-9ab7-55d3-84fd-14bcb386f979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{t^{3}}-\\frac{4t^{3}}{3}-3t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681144} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f7a97e-11e8-5f16-ac5c-8bf113da83fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{t^{3}}}{3}-\\frac{2t^{3}}{3}-3t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6407384-9ab7-55d3-84fd-14bcb386f979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{t^{3}}-\\frac{4t^{3}}{3}-3t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681144} :answer-field/id #uuid "e94871a4-cc38-549b-9089-40f0053b3d0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7dbfae1c-a39c-5316-9573-87647a29f09c" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -6\\sqrt{t}-4t^{2}-3\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. Assume $t>0$." :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -6\\sqrt{t}-4t^{2}-3\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-6t^{\\frac{1}{2}}-4t^{2}-3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -6\\int t^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-4\\int t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-3\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -6\\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right)-4\\left(\\frac{t^{3}}{3}\\right)-3\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -4\\sqrt{t^{3}}-\\frac{4t^{3}}{3}-3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186690772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7f1153-e0ad-508c-accf-9573362f16e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d814cb95fc479c8162bae948aae3de632a83fbd3427485168dca34275639a96e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690764} {:answer/id #uuid "18e35990-a7c5-5b85-8dbb-86000c7f7843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a69a759f1111ffe3bec12085682edfc2878c769d6c09fd07aedc7882ca0c875e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690765} {:answer/id #uuid "57fe66c4-3c61-53cc-8e20-598186f1d08e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/0173e3c9f1f5774da5d4983a4451072c40178f5bff0c72b86646f9ec01e0a32d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690771} {:answer/id #uuid "d0688139-536c-524d-8631-5522b4d0deb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8ac88b2a6755ad1c9bdea73dbdef746f8a3a787cc2ba27878bc0c14b3bdf5e35.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690779} {:answer/id #uuid "f922fb40-12a4-5ad0-a125-9a0003869c7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b3/b34511426ca7682e46c0def9dc04cbcc5995f4f81f1e1f43ca7062e6c8ba08ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57fe66c4-3c61-53cc-8e20-598186f1d08e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/0173e3c9f1f5774da5d4983a4451072c40178f5bff0c72b86646f9ec01e0a32d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690771} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c514f8d-1276-5d10-be37-ef97c5c5064c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6977afe1-7dc8-5ee0-b49f-4521c79d6ddd" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y$, which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=f(x,y)$?" :question/worked-solution "We must find the field that fails to depend on both variables. In field C, moving left or right along any horizontal row leaves the slope unchanged. The slope depends on $y$ alone, so field C cannot represent the stated dependence.\n\n![](images/f6/f6beb35791eba0528c5a19688995752454ff168f83d94d644fe481011f4387b0.png)\n\nIn fields A, B, D, and E, slopes change along horizontal rows and along vertical columns, so none can be excluded by the equation type alone.\n\nOne constant row or column is not enough to rule out dependence on both variables. For example, field A has horizontal segments throughout $y=0$, yet its other rows change with $x$ and its nonzero columns change with $y$. Field D likewise has a constant row at $y=1$ and a constant column at $x=-1$.\n\nTherefore, choose field C. We do not need to find a formula or solve a differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186690773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29ebf7ef-9c58-51bb-8797-1b4837a7c50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/45/45ae6cdd661f1fc2528328556e84ee64d9e2bf097891a538db1f7111e8892439.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690766} {:answer/id #uuid "55179e26-6e74-5d7d-9c00-295cd53251ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2fb81f8d65fc55ad945807640522a0551f865921b889e7aef2672b2322d221ca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690770} {:answer/id #uuid "745b1b2f-2486-55ff-86b6-05ddf3d6bb12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8d/8db24d57369b9cf5e2e604263120c1f2b4d47dbf1ef70b5e6b7175f5cd337fca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690775} {:answer/id #uuid "97b9aa57-1d55-56d9-9d89-e949dc8b4a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7fee724b0567eb7ede117a16906342e78f97cf0ad10d43513c42dea434bacc18.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690777} {:answer/id #uuid "cea5fd8a-b98a-5263-a5eb-a45f17efb45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d4d2a49dbab2bcb8bb0aff760f20a42e6aec1ce5f63c6f1f5a9658e0c8353bc4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55179e26-6e74-5d7d-9c00-295cd53251ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2fb81f8d65fc55ad945807640522a0551f865921b889e7aef2672b2322d221ca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690770} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b9b9b2-f953-5c4c-b9df-edbc618ed039" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70f49580-a737-5502-9cf5-686a089ba362" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x-y^2$?" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side depends on both $x$ and $y$. We need a field whose slopes change with $x$ when $y$ is fixed, and also change with $y$ when $x$ is fixed.\n\n| Field | Down a column | Across a row |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A | Same | Changes |\n| B | Changes | Same |\n| C | Same | Changes |\n| D | Changes | Changes |\n| E | Changes | Same |\n\nOnly field D has the required dependence on both variables. The blue comparison lines below make the changes visible: along $y=1$, the slope is $x-1$; along $x=1$, it is $1-y^2$. The equal slopes at $y=-1$ and $y=1$ do not mean that the slope is constant throughout that column.\n\n![](images/e5/e509bd802b3e32716dff57d5c7c7e49f8985b223fe05ab21add91f68f995857f.png)\n\nTherefore, choose field D."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186690781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cbf4e3d-35c6-5371-bebd-e7080135bb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/00/002404d24b64b3c4b1de9d401c9856ec8f8c10aa8ef3d74a8ae4c597f994373a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690763} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb61e85-62c3-5555-a1aa-116ae984c707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6f2eaa083b1a25f035dda6b67fd3e2d858cc2ed4568723a42b33b149e2116f09.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690767} {:answer/id #uuid "498a023d-dac4-5279-93ce-f0431fe0a25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a5/a549678cca02fffc4a0200524f73281e3c61466c7027ba540b590f5bc643dbc7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "498a023d-dac4-5279-93ce-f0431fe0a25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a5/a549678cca02fffc4a0200524f73281e3c61466c7027ba540b590f5bc643dbc7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690768} :answer-field/id #uuid "72c913cd-dc1f-56d9-a85e-d04a83b3480b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f9fb99c1-a717-508c-96c2-329620d3ab48" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=x+y$?\n\nCompare the slopes along the blue lines $x=1$ and $y=1$. The right-hand side changes when either variable changes while the other is held fixed. Choose the field showing both kinds of change." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side depends on both variables. Along $x=1$, the slope is $1+y$, so it changes as we move up or down. Along $y=1$, it is $x+1$, so it changes as we move left or right.\n\nField B shows both changes. Field A repeats the same slope across each horizontal row, while field C repeats the same slope along each vertical column. They depend on only one variable.\n\n![](images/a5/a549678cca02fffc4a0200524f73281e3c61466c7027ba540b590f5bc643dbc7.png)\n\nTherefore, choose field B."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54017e16-266c-596d-bce1-c9f313332365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/131380aead2a93e1c578cf44185d4f51a911d4dfdd18a9e772ece2baa9fd81c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693301} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9ad31e-b79e-50d2-ad4a-b25ccba68543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/21ecd9bbd6b6071525fdd96ac0f6d047067ef9065e494f8691d090d943edd23a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693303} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4d5e01-fa53-5cdf-97f8-18daceddfbf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/94a6d59a2a396b63f58638ccd4527979c3294e176bd20b5b8de13695145da748.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693305} {:answer/id #uuid "e22b176b-94ba-5a1a-aef0-883492ea7df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9e237b1ed82702a83a9dd57bae1b8b145869b6553a3d1d3cf46bdb462f1185c1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693306} {:answer/id #uuid "f27e6800-00e7-5466-ace8-46e24046f63e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8b5c5f4e806859fac4d92e66e8bfff087e2678cd8d801783f01f9abd4b3dee3f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e22b176b-94ba-5a1a-aef0-883492ea7df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9e237b1ed82702a83a9dd57bae1b8b145869b6553a3d1d3cf46bdb462f1185c1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693306} :answer-field/id #uuid "70fd8397-b324-5fa4-8323-4ea38cdcc6c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "58085d0c-d532-5e36-a1b6-bf3108d7a472" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $y$ given a fixed value of $x.$\n\n![](images/9e/9e237b1ed82702a83a9dd57bae1b8b145869b6553a3d1d3cf46bdb462f1185c1.png)\n\n \n\nThe slopes repeat along every vertical line, not just the marked example. The other fields show variation in both directions somewhere. A single constant row or column would not be enough to exclude them.\n\n![](images/9e/9ea58ec780f3197256943f3a1ed62537364653e563ed214b33b6b716ec3bb19d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e51d514-fde3-57b6-a880-e3ce506326ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/80f8885caa92b9773633151a7980e89865fb7d4ef8b8f96527c7124812cabb6a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693314} {:answer/id #uuid "55d9f16f-7fb2-50ba-a610-24ad70e8642e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9efa2eb55289f062aa45c057329aae275d0862e93481b4a9e93412c455bd875f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693316} {:answer/id #uuid "dbaabbe8-1129-594f-8bd5-eaf7170ffc5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f9/f974f1ce57363500b88c953a1129287df42e9fe895d7ba0a699715b0dbbaa834.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55d9f16f-7fb2-50ba-a610-24ad70e8642e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9efa2eb55289f062aa45c057329aae275d0862e93481b4a9e93412c455bd875f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693316} :answer-field/id #uuid "77b4fefd-0483-57d4-97e7-23e40e6b0f5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186693319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0331e6f9-5aa9-5fc1-a79d-a4bf24126ae5" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- x - y - 1?$\n\nCompare the slopes along the blue lines $x=0.75$ and $y=-0.75$. The right-hand side changes when either variable changes while the other is held fixed. Choose the field showing both kinds of change." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/9e/9efa2eb55289f062aa45c057329aae275d0862e93481b4a9e93412c455bd875f.png)\n\n \n\nAlong the blue comparison lines, slopes change in both directions. A special constant row or column does not rule out dependence on both variables; check the rest of the field as well.\n\n![](images/9e/9efa2eb55289f062aa45c057329aae275d0862e93481b4a9e93412c455bd875f.png)"}]}
